      <h2>What Does General Liability Insurance Actually Cover?</h2>
      <p>
        A standard ISO Commercial General Liability form (CG 00 01) protects your business against three distinct categories of third-party claims:
      </p>

      <h3>1. Third-Party Bodily Injury</h3>
      <p>
        If a client, delivery courier, or vendor visits your premises and slips on an icy walkway or freshly waxed floor, general liability pays for their emergency medical care, diagnostic imaging, lost wages, and your legal defense team.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Third-Party Property Damage</h3>
      <p>
        If your employee visits a customer’s commercial facility to install computer workstations and accidentally knocks over an expensive industrial printer, your policy compensates the client for the replacement value.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Personal and Advertising Injury</h3>
      <p>
        In an era of intensive digital marketing, general liability covers defense costs if your company is sued for libel, slander, unintentional copyright infringement in advertising campaigns, or violation of a competitor's trademark slogan.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout-warning">
        <div class="callout-title">⚠️ What General Liability Does NOT Cover</div>
        <p class="callout-text">
          General liability does NOT cover employee workplace injuries (requires <strong>Workers' Compensation</strong>), professional advice errors or software coding bugs (requires <strong>Errors & Omissions / Professional Liability</strong>), damage to your own company vehicles (requires <strong>Commercial Auto</strong>), or customer data leaks (requires <strong>Cyber Liability</strong>).
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Decoding Policy Limits: What Does "$1M / $2M" Mean?</h2>
      <p>
        When you receive a commercial insurance certificate of liability (COI), you will almost invariably see limits expressed as <strong>$1,000,000 / $2,000,000</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Occurrence Limit ($1,000,000):</strong> The maximum amount the insurance carrier will pay for any single accident, claim, or incident during your 12-month policy term.</li>
        <li><strong>General Aggregate Limit ($2,000,000):</strong> The absolute maximum total payout the insurer will provide across ALL combined claims occurring during the policy year. Once this ceiling is exhausted, the insurer will pay no further claims until your policy renews.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Business Owner’s Policy (BOP): The Smart Small Business Bundle</h2>
      <p>
        If you operate a qualifying small business (typically under $5 million in gross revenue and fewer than 50 employees), you should request a <strong>Business Owner’s Policy (BOP)</strong> rather than purchasing standalone general liability.
      </p>
      <p>A BOP bundles three indispensable coverages into one unified contract:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>General Liability:</strong> Covers third-party injury, property damage, and advertising claims;</li>
        <li><strong>Commercial Property Insurance:</strong> Protects your owned or leased building, computers, inventory, tools, and office furnishings against fire, theft, and storm damage;</li>
        <li><strong>Business Interruption Coverage:</strong> Replaces lost net operating income and ongoing payroll if a covered peril (like a fire) forces your operations to close temporarily for repairs.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>5 Actionable Ways to Reduce Commercial Insurance Premiums</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Implement Written Workplace Safety Protocols:</strong> Insurers offer safety credits when you document formal slip-and-fall prevention procedures, spill cleanup checklists, and ladder safety training.</li>
        <li><strong>Require Certificates of Insurance (COIs) from Subcontractors:</strong> If you hire independent contractors without verifying they carry their own general liability insurance, your insurer will classify their payouts as internal payroll during your year-end audit, assessing massive retroactive premiums.</li>
        <li><strong>Accurately Estimate Annual Revenue & Payroll:</strong> Never drastically inflate revenue estimates on your application. Set realistic expectations, as your carrier will adjust your rate at the annual policy audit.</li>
        <li><strong>Select Higher Deductibles on Property Endorsements:</strong> Choosing a $2,500 property deductible instead of $500 lowers your monthly BOP premium by up to 15%.</li>
        <li><strong>Join Industry Trade Associations:</strong> Many carrier groups offer 5% to 10% premium reductions for active members of recognized state trade associations and chambers of commerce.</li>
      </ol>

      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <div class="faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Business Liability</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">What is a Certificate of Insurance (COI) and why do clients demand it? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            A Certificate of Insurance (COI) is a single-page document (usually an ACORD 25 form) issued by your insurance broker that proves you hold an active policy with required limits. Commercial clients and landlords require COIs—often demanding to be named as an "Additional Insured"—before allowing you on job sites or signing contracts to ensure your insurer pays first if an accident occurs.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Do single-member LLCs or sole proprietors really need general liability? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Yes. While an LLC provides legal separation between business and personal assets, courts can "pierce the corporate veil" if negligence or tort liability is established. A $500,000 slip-and-fall judgment would instantly bankrupt an uninsured small LLC and disrupt operations permanently.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">What is an annual commercial insurance premium audit? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            At the conclusion of each 12-month commercial policy term, the insurance carrier performs an audit of your tax records, payroll statements, and 1099 disbursements. If your business grew faster than initially estimated, you will owe an additional premium; if your revenue or payroll decreased, you will receive a refund.
          </div>
        </div>
